r. They're arrogant.&nbsp; As you know, I worked for the Industrial Bank of&nbsp;&nbsp; Japan Trust Company in the late 80s. And most of&nbsp; my colleagues were sent to the United States and&nbsp;&nbsp; all claimed that they spoke fluent English.&nbsp; Like the people you described, they couldn't&nbsp;&nbsp; speak English for [ __ ]. They could not. And&nbsp; it was like if an American didn't understand,&nbsp;&nbsp; like a waiter, what they wanted, they would be&nbsp; like, that person is stupid. And I'd be like,&nbsp;&nbsp; you can't speak English. And they used the word a&nbsp; lot. In fact, I wasn't going to tell this story,&nbsp;&nbsp; but I will now that you say that. The new&nbsp; Japanese ambassador in Bahrain had just arrived&nbsp;&nbsp; from Chicago. This was 1994 or 1995. And so we get&nbsp; this invitation that the ambassador is inviting&nbsp;&nbsp; our ambassador, our deputy chief of mission, our&nbsp; political officer, our economic officer, and our&nbsp;&nbsp; commercial officer—so five of us—to dinner at&nbsp; his residence. And one thing about the Japanese,&nbsp;&nbsp; when they have a dinner, it's no holds barred.&nbsp; It's caviar and the most expensive steaks. It's&nbsp;&nbsp; incredible. They're like, hey, we're going to have&nbsp; dinner at the Japanese ambassador's residence.&nbsp;&nbsp; This is going to be great. So we go there.And&nbsp; we're all just kind of standing around. And the&nbsp;&nbsp; younger guys spoke some English. The ambassador&nbsp; didn't speak a word. Well, barely a word,&nbsp;&nbsp; as you'll see in a moment. And so we finally sit&nbsp; down after an hour of chit-chat, pleasantries.&nbsp;&nbsp; We sit down at the dining-room table and our&nbsp; ambassador says, “So, Mr. Ambassador, to what do&nbsp;&nbsp; we owe this pleasure?” And the Japanese ambassador&nbsp; says, “Your president, he's stupid.”It turned out&nbsp;&nbsp; that when he was in Chicago, he somehow struck&nbsp; up this acquaintanceship with Dan Quayle, who had&