t. So he made a&nbsp; pretextual excuse to have a business meeting with&nbsp;&nbsp; the president of the bank because he heard the&nbsp; painting was in the bank president's office—that&nbsp;&nbsp; they had put it there. So we go over there and&nbsp; he was always scheming like this. So we had no&nbsp;&nbsp; business really with them. We just wanted to be in&nbsp; there for 15 minutes just to see the painting.So&nbsp;&nbsp; we go over there and sure enough, there it&nbsp; is. And it was hanging over the radiator,&nbsp;&nbsp; right? Like directly above where the heat's coming&nbsp; out, directly underneath. We didn't even—my boss&nbsp;&nbsp; was brilliant, like you. I loved working for&nbsp; this guy. He was my best boss ever. And Shibuya&nbsp;&nbsp; looks at me, I look at him, we don't even have&nbsp; to say a word to each other. I'm just like,&nbsp;&nbsp; holy shit.We walk out of there and I remember he&nbsp; just says, “Disgusting.” He's like, the Japanese&nbsp;&nbsp; will get their comeuppance and we're [ __ ].&nbsp; Because it was like they were flying high in the&nbsp;&nbsp; 80s and buying everything big-time. We thought&nbsp; the U.S. was done. Yeah, we did. Remember there&nbsp;&nbsp; were those movies about American workers having&nbsp; to learn how to do jumping jacks in the morning&nbsp;&nbsp; at the factories and stuff like that.I'm glad that&nbsp; they would not have been good overlords.So yeah.&nbsp;&nbsp; What do you want to talk about first? Because&nbsp; we're getting a late start here. Well, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp; we should say real quickly that Saudi Crown Prince&nbsp; Mohammed bin Salman is in Washington today. So&nbsp;&nbsp; this sort of completes his rehabilitation&nbsp; after ordering the murder and dismemberment&nbsp;&nbsp; of a Washington Post journalist.Trump loves the&nbsp; Saudis. The Saudis love Trump. You can bet your&nbsp;&nbsp; bottom dollar that the Trump family is going to&nbsp; be wealthier at the end of the day today than it&nbsp;&nb