nbsp; here. Does anyone with Trump's ear tell him&nbsp; that from the CIA? I doubt it. Because remember,&nbsp;&nbsp; most of the time the CIA director is a political&nbsp; appointee and they're really not thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp; these longer-term issues.It does bug me what a&nbsp; profit center they are. I think it was Harvard&nbsp;&nbsp; that said that 27% of all of their undergraduate&nbsp; enrollment was international students. And think&nbsp;&nbsp; about how incredibly competitive it is to&nbsp; attend Harvard. I mean, literally one out&nbsp;&nbsp; of four spots there is taken by foreigners. I&nbsp; can't think that one out of four spots at the&nbsp;&nbsp; Sorbonne or the University of Tokyo are taken by&nbsp; foreign students. Oxford—I don't think so. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp; yes. Okay, good point.Anything else to say about&nbsp; that before we move on? No, just that it could be&nbsp;&nbsp; a very important operational opportunity lost.This&nbsp; is a funny comment. I'm just going to read it.&nbsp;&nbsp; Tzoli says, “I want my adult stepson to move&nbsp; out so I can experience time with my husband&nbsp;&nbsp; before I die. Can I get that somewhere on Black&nbsp; Friday? He's only 36.” But I don't feel badly one&nbsp;&nbsp; little bit. Only 36. Don't worry. Once they hit&nbsp; 42 they fly the nest. It'll be fine.It's really&nbsp;&nbsp; funny though, but that's the new economy.Oh, this&nbsp; is a good question from Rodngu9864. Thanks for the&nbsp;&nbsp; two euros. “Can Trump and Mamdani find a way to&nbsp; coexist?” I'm glad you brought up that question&nbsp;&nbsp; because there's reporting today that says that&nbsp; Mamdani, his team asked Trump's team for a meeting&nbsp;&nbsp; and Trump said that he's amenable to sitting&nbsp; down with Mamdani. John, what do you make of the&nbsp;&nbsp; fact that all the people who came out so harshly&nbsp; against Mamdani, the communist who was going to&nbsp;&nbsp; bring Marxist-Leninism to the five boroughs—now&nbsp; you have your favorite Gristedes Greek grocer—he&nbsp;&nbsp; says he's not only not leaving the city, he wants&nbsp; people to cooperate with Mamdani so that things&nbsp;&nbsp; work out really well. Trump, who had all sorts of&nbsp; shitty things to say about Mamdani, is now saying,&nbsp;&nbsp; oh, I'll meet with him. Governor Hochul, who&nbsp; refused to endorse him until the last second—I'm&nbsp;&nbsp; still waiting, Hillary Clinton might endorse&nbsp; Mamdani halfway through his term. But I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp; all these establishment—mean, Mamdani's wielding&nbsp; a lot of power. So it's a two-part question:&nbsp;&nbsp; how do they look in all this? And then how can&nbsp; voters take any of this seriously when they hear&nbsp;&nbsp; this person is evil and terrifying and far-left&nbsp; and communist? Oh hey, welcome to the White House.&nbsp;&nbsp; How's it going?Yeah, right. Ted, you and I are&nbsp; both old enough to remember when the Democratic&nbsp;&nbsp; Party was a big-tent party, when everybody from&nbsp; Ted Kennedy to George Wallace were Democrats,&nbsp;&nbsp; when almost every Senate seat in every southern&nbsp; state was held by a conservative Democrat—and I&nbsp;&nbsp; mean conservative Democrat. And the Democrats&nbsp; claim that they're still a big-tent party and&nbsp;&nbsp; they're not. Now they are so middle-of-the-road&nbsp; that they're just stuck. It's like being stuck in&nbsp;&nbsp; neutral, where they don't want these conservatives&nbsp; but they also don't want these liberals either.&nbsp;&nbsp; And it's going to end up being the demise of the&nbsp; Democratic Party.Well, I mean the question is if&nbsp;&nbsp; the Republican Party doesn't fall apart first. I&nbsp; mean the civil war between the Trump wing and the&nbsp;&nbsp; MTG wing is really heating up. Right now I gotta&nbsp; say I didn't expect to see this. Trump is still&nbsp;&nbsp; such a canny politician. Like even the way that&nbsp; he's pivoting on the Epstein files—it's really&nbsp;&nbsp; a masterclass in many ways, but even though it's&nbsp; cynical. But I don't know that he's winning the&nbsp;&nbsp; war with MTG right now. She's the one who's coming&nbsp; out smelling like roses. They both backed off&nbsp;&nbsp; yesterday but she has won this little kerfuffle.&nbsp; Her reputation has improved dramatically. I think&nbsp;&nbsp; the real secret sauce was admitting that she had&nbsp; contributed to the toxic atmosphere and expressing&nbsp;&nbsp; regret for it. I don't know about you, John—I'm a&nbsp; huge Catholic, I'm a huge sucker for penance and&nbsp;&nbsp; repentance. If someone's an [ __ ] and they say,&nbsp; oh God I was an [ __ ] and I shouldn't have been,&nbsp;&nbsp; I'm sorry—I'm like, I got nothing. You win. That's&nbsp; because that's all you can ask from people. They&nbsp;&nbsp; can't go back in time. I agree. I agree.Robby&nbsp; is saying that Tucker has doubled down. Tucker&nbsp;&nbsp; has doubled down. You know, and it's funny, people&nbsp; ask me all the time what I make of the new Tucker&nbsp;&nbsp; Carlson. And all I can say is the guy answers to&nbsp; nobody. And when you don't work for anybody but&nbsp;&nbsp; yourself and you're not doing it for the money,&nbsp; you can say anything that your heart desires. And&nbsp;&nbsp; we're seeing a totally liberated and free Tucker&nbsp; Carlson right now. I can't even imagine—speaking&nbsp;&nbsp; of which, when he was at Fox, how many memos came&nbsp; down like, okay, so our point on this topic or&nbsp;&nbsp; this person is this. Don't deviate from that. I&nbsp; mean I know internally I've heard about how MSNBC,&nbsp;&nbsp; NBC did that all the time. They sent the word down&nbsp; like—you know Ed Schultz, right? He was told in&nbsp;&nbsp; no uncertain terms, you can't discuss Bernie&nbsp; Sanders during the Democratic primary. Like&nbsp;&nbsp; really? You can't talk about a primary candidate.&nbsp; Ed was a friend of mine and we ended up working&nbsp;&nbsp; at RT together. He never forgave NBC for doing&nbsp; that. I never forgave them.Well, it's disgusting.&nbsp;&nbsp; And of course my agent told me that MSNBC was&nbsp; interested in trying me out late night on a&nbsp;&nbsp; weekend. And they said the only thing I had to&nbsp; promise was to never criticize the Democratic&nbsp;&nbsp; Party. And I remember what I said. I said, well&nbsp; tell them when it's like France and there's 26&nbsp;&nbsp; parties and if there's one out of the 26 that I&nbsp; can't criticize, that's fine. But there's only&nbsp;&nbsp; two. So how can you do your job? It wasn't even&nbsp; a matter of integrity. It was literally from a&nbsp;&nbsp; practical standpoint I don't know how you can do&nbsp; this without sounding ridiculous. And the truth is&nbsp;&nbsp; you can't. They do sound ridiculous.I want to say&nbsp; two things. First of all, Morrow, thank you very&nbsp;&nbsp; much for the donation, $4.99. Appreciate that&nbsp; very much. And then James Jamel—hey James—says&nbsp;&nbsp; John would love Lincoln stovepipe hat for&nbsp; Christmas. You know the stovepipe hat is on&nbsp;&nbsp; display in the Smithsonian's Museum of American&nbsp; History. It's an incredible thing to see. But I&nbsp;&nbsp; will add that he must have had several because&nbsp; the one that he was wearing the night that he&nbsp;&nbsp; was killed is the one that's there. He actually&nbsp; had taken it off and had put it on a table next to&nbsp;&nbsp; him. So there's not like a hole in it or anything.&nbsp; Because a gentleman didn't wear a hat inside.&nbsp;&nbsp; That's right. That still bugs me. I'm old enough&nbsp; that when I see dudes wearing baseball caps inside&nbsp;&nbsp; I'm like, take that [ __ ] thing off, asshole.So&nbsp; this past week I spent in Oxford, Ohio with my son&nbsp;&nbsp; Max, my third son, and Max loves antique malls&nbsp; as much as I do. So we drove to Indianapolis,&nbsp;&nbsp; which isn't too terribly far away—about two hours&nbsp; from where he goes to school. Boring, boring,&nbsp;&nbsp; boring, farm after farm after farm. It's enough&nbsp; to make you want to cut your throat. Anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp; there's this one dealer that we like that I've&nbsp; bought stuff from before because he has impeccable&nbsp;&nbsp; provenance for everything that he sells. And&nbsp; I bought a hunk of wood from the gallows where&nbsp;&nbsp; the Lincoln conspirators were hanged. And it&nbsp; includes a scan of a handwritten letter dated 1889&nbsp;&nbsp; from the soldier who was one of the members of&nbsp; the crew that took the gallows down. And he kept&nbsp;&nbsp; one of the planks and then divided it up to give&nbsp; to friends.You know there's all these—when they&nbsp;&nbsp; used to hang people the gallows often seem to be&nbsp; improvised. They didn't have a permanent facility.&nbsp;&nbsp; In this case it's because people weren't normally&nbsp; hanged at this base. The base still exists—it's&nbsp;&nbsp; now Joint Base Anacostia-Bolling or they call it&nbsp; Joint Base something or other. They just changed&nbsp;&nbsp; all the names a couple of years ago. Now the site&nbsp; of the gallows is the officers' tennis courts. So&nbsp;&nbsp; there's no trace that these gallows were there&nbsp; and that they hanged the Lincoln conspirators,&nbsp;&nbsp; including the first woman to be executed by the&nbsp; U.S. government. There was a woman conspirator.&nbsp;&nbsp; Yeah, Mary Surratt. She ran the boarding house.&nbsp; She plotted with them and she's the one that&nbsp;&nbsp; hid the gun that John Wilkes Booth used. She&nbsp; hid it behind the stove in her kitchen.Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp; so I definitely have a wish list for&nbsp; my political ephemera collection.&nbsp;&nbsp; All I'm lacking now is I want a pardon signed&nbsp; by Abraham Lincoln. They're out there. They go&nbsp;&nbsp; for about ten to fourteen thousand dollars. I'm&nbsp; not in a position to buy something like that,&nbsp;&nbsp; but I'm hoping that 2026 turns out to be a really&nbsp; great year for me in which case I'm going to grab&nbsp;&nbsp; one. Frankly I'd rather you got a pardon signed&nbsp; by President Trump, but that's just me. Me too.&nbsp;&nbsp; Although the Lincoln one sounds cool too.All&nbsp; right. So we should probably talk about the&nbsp;&nbsp; situation in Israel. Let's first get into the UN&nbsp; Security Council. So we all know, we talked here&nbsp;&nbsp; on the show about the 20-point plan. And if you're&nbsp; watching this show you probably have a pretty good&nbsp;&nbsp; idea of what's in it. But as I mentioned, John,&nbsp; this is not a plan that seems to have enough meat&nbsp;&nbsp; on the bones to make a lot of sense. Okay, so&nbsp; there's a peacekeeping force—that I understand.&nbsp;&nbsp; It's going to be a bunch of Muslim countries,&nbsp; including Pakistan—that I understand too. But who&nbsp;&nbsp; are they keeping the peace from? They're going to&nbsp; disarm Hamas? How? Hamas says they don't want to&nbsp;&nbsp; be disarmed. They had no seat at the table here.&nbsp; They called this an international guardianship&nbsp;&nbsp; mechanism, kind of reminiscent of the French&nbsp; mandate over Lebanon and that kind of thing.&nbsp;&nbsp; I don't think they're wrong. Then, you know,&nbsp; basically this is a non-contiguous state. Israel,&nbsp;&nbsp; by the way, just parenthetically rolling this&nbsp; back—Hamas justified October 7th by saying if they&nbsp;&nbsp; hadn't done something like this they would have&nbsp; never achieved statehood. Now Netanyahu says we&nbsp;&nbsp; will never agree to the creation of a Palestinian&nbsp; state, retroactively basically confirming what&nbsp;&nbsp; Hamas said and kind of justifying October 7th&nbsp; after the fact. It's so easy to see all the&nbsp;&nbsp; ways this can't work. And yet this agreement&nbsp; now has the force of international law.Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp; it does. Is it a dead letter or are they going&nbsp; to try to do this?Let's try to parse it out.&nbsp;&nbsp; There is no meat on the bone when it comes to the&nbsp; issue of Palestinian statehood. So the vote was 13&nbsp;&nbsp; to nothing. China and Russia abstained just like&nbsp; we predicted that they would. But almost all of&nbsp;&nbsp; the council members—France, Guyana, Pakistan,&nbsp; Slovenia, Somalia—complained that there was no&nbsp;&nbsp; language about an independent Palestinian&nbsp; state. And that's where we're going to get&nbsp;&nbsp; wrapped around the axle again. Everybody agrees&nbsp; that there needs to be peace, there needs to be&nbsp;&nbsp; respect for Palestinian human rights, et cetera,&nbsp; et cetera. But Benjamin Netanyahu said—and I have&nbsp;&nbsp; the quote right here from the New York Times—“Our&nbsp; opposition to a Palestinian state in any territory&nbsp;&nbsp; has not changed. There will be no independent&nbsp; Palestinian state if it's up to the Israelis ever,&nbsp;&nbsp; in any configuration.” So you can't be forced&nbsp; down their throats is the question. Or will it&nbsp;&nbsp; be?It can be, but will it be—not as things sit&nbsp; today. Now can the Israelis just be sidelined&nbsp;&nbsp; at a certain point? Because international&nbsp; relationships rely on cooperation. If the Israelis&nbsp;&nbsp; are uncooperative maybe you just don't work with&nbsp; them. And what do you think of this, Ted? Is this&nbsp;&nbsp; just an accident or is Donald Trump a genius here&nbsp; where you've got all these Arab countries—Egypt,&nbsp;&nbsp; the United Arab Emirates, Tunisia and other&nbsp; Muslim countries, Indonesia, Turkey—that have&nbsp;&nbsp; agreed to send troops, these peacekeeping troops,&nbsp; but they're very afraid of their troops getting&nbsp;&nbsp; into an armed conflict with Hamas. So you're going&nbsp; to have Muslim armies shooting Hamas paramilitary&nbsp;&nbsp; forces. Arabs killing Arabs, Muslims killing&nbsp; Muslims. Is that what Donald Trump planned or did&nbsp;&nbsp; it just kind of work out that way?I don't think&nbsp; that's what he wants. I think he's really kind of&nbsp;&nbsp; hoping that Hamas will not fire at them because&nbsp; they're Muslims and that they'll be—I mean look,&nbsp;&nbsp; another question is do you think Hamas will handle&nbsp; this the way the Afghans handle an invasion? You&nbsp;&nbsp; know, they go to ground, the Afghans go to the&nbsp; mountains, they study the enemy, then they do&nbsp;&nbsp; probing attacks and eventually they come back&nbsp; and flood the field and the invaders never knew&nbsp;&nbsp; what hit them. Obviously there's no mountains in&nbsp; Gaza to hide in and they can't even hide in those&nbsp;&nbsp; tunnels forever. But basically they can take&nbsp; off their uniforms and slip back into civilian&nbsp;&nbsp; life and just wait to get the call from their&nbsp; commanders, right? It's a cell structure. It's&nbsp;&nbsp; a classic resistance organization. Classic. So&nbsp; will they just sort of, okay guys, go back home,&nbsp;&nbsp; try to rebuild the best you can, and we'll outlast&nbsp; and outwait these Pakistanis and so on, and then&nbsp;&nbsp; we'll get our country back.I think it very well&nbsp; could. The bottom line though is what we said a&nbsp;&nbsp; minute ago: the Israelis are going to have to be&nbsp; forced into this. And it really, we do need to&nbsp;&nbsp; force the Israelis into this. There has to be an&nbsp; independent Palestine. There must be. There has to&nbsp;&nbsp; be. For Israel's own good. And what an independent&nbsp; Palestine was called for in the Security Council&nbsp;&nbsp; resolution that created the state of Israel in&nbsp; the first place. Reasonable Israelis all know&nbsp;&nbsp; this is essential for their own security. Exactly&nbsp; right. And by the way the non-contiguity—nobody is&nbsp;&nbsp; addressing the contiguity. The fact that it's&nbsp; not [ __ ] attached. That's a problem. As a&nbsp;&nbsp; Pakistani, whether that's a problem or not.&nbsp; Yeah, that's right. Now they made it work in&nbsp;&nbsp; Bosnia and Herzegovina, but that was an unusual&nbsp; situation. So the Israelis don't see them granting&nbsp;&nbsp; a corridor, but they have to. You have to. I guess&nbsp; a bridge, right, like a big long bridge like the&nbsp;&nbsp; way they have over the Florida Keys so that the&nbsp; Israelis can go underneath and stuff. Oh my God I&nbsp;&nbsp; can't even imagine—it's got to be something. Well,&nbsp; it's not that far. I think before that happens&nbsp;&nbsp; we're going to see the proposed Trump-Putin&nbsp; bridge-tunnel connecting Siberia with Alaska.&nbsp;&nbsp; I would actually drive it just to say I did. I&nbsp; also think it's a really good idea for political&nbsp;&nbsp; and economic reasons. It would be great for&nbsp; American business to have access to Siberia. And&nbsp;&nbsp; it would be fun.You know what I did with my kids&nbsp; a couple of Christmases ago—Christmas I find very,&nbsp;&nbsp; very depressing and I don't decorate, I don't&nbsp; put up any tree or decorations or anything, just&nbsp;&nbsp; pretend it's a normal day like every other day,&nbsp; just cause I get too depressed to even function.&nbsp;&nbsp; How come you always were you like that as a kid?&nbsp; No, Christmas was wonderful when I was a kid,&nbsp;&nbsp; but I'm divorced twice and it's just not—I just&nbsp; can't do it. So what I did with the kids a couple&nbsp;&nbsp; of years ago, I said hey let's open your presents&nbsp; and then let's get on the subway and we'll go to&nbsp;&nbsp; every single station on the entire subway system&nbsp; in New York City. Oh you can do it in New York&nbsp;&nbsp; City. There was an article about it in the Times&nbsp; and a guy did it in 25 hours, which is record&nbsp;&nbsp; fast. I was going to say because I've been to&nbsp; every station in the New York City subway system—I&nbsp;&nbsp; used to explore it in college—and you'd have to,&nbsp; it would take a long time, lot of planning, lot&nbsp;&nbsp; of backtracking. But anyway we did it in nine and&nbsp; a half hours and we did take a break. We actually&nbsp;&nbsp; came up out of the station at the zoo and we had&nbsp; lunch and then we went back down and finished&nbsp;&nbsp; it.We have similar impulses. I'm not a big fan&nbsp; of the holidays either. I hate Thanksgiving more&nbsp;&nbsp; than Christmas. And I once spent Christmas Day&nbsp; with two friends exploring the abandoned subway&nbsp;&nbsp; tunnels under Cincinnati. I don't know if you&nbsp; know about that but there was a subway. In fact&nbsp;&nbsp; my son and I were just talking about it the day&nbsp; before yesterday. Cincinnati has more abandoned&nbsp;&nbsp; subway tunnels than any other city in America. I&nbsp; bet it's not as accessible as it used to be, but&nbsp;&nbsp; back in the day you could just—there was a place&nbsp; in a public park where someone had broken in and&nbsp;&nbsp; you could just walk in there. Bring a flashlight&nbsp; and plenty of batteries and you could walk like&nbsp;&nbsp; 12 miles. It was really crazy. And there's&nbsp; fallout shelters in there from the Cold War,&nbsp;&nbsp; including with a podium where people were going&nbsp; to address the crowd. There's a blocked arsenal&nbsp;&nbsp; that you couldn't break into. It was really cool.&nbsp; And in the end finally you sort of like, there's a&nbsp;&nbsp; ladder, like oh I think this is the way out. Climb&nbsp; the ladder and there's a manhole cover and you&nbsp;&nbsp; lift the manhole and there's cars going over—it's&nbsp; like oh, better time this carefully.Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp; it was cool.So I do think that Trump—to get&nbsp; back to your main question though—he is kind&nbsp;&nbsp; of a genius in that as [ __ ] up as this plan is,&nbsp; this is progress, right? I mean I don't think we'd&nbsp;&nbsp; be here under President Biden or President Harris.&nbsp; The Israelis would still be bombing. I agree with&nbsp;&nbsp; you. As pro-Israel and pro-Zionist as Donald Trump&nbsp; is, he expects people to do as they're told. And&nbsp;&nbsp; Netanyahu won't do as he's told. And so you've&nbsp; got to force him. Yeah, that's the thing about&nbsp;&nbsp; Trump. He does understand power and leverage.&nbsp; It's the only thing he really understands, but he&nbsp;&nbsp; understands them really well.I still have to say&nbsp; I'm in complete agreement with Tucker Carlson and&nbsp;&nbsp; his rant about Israel is inconsequential. We don't&nbsp; need them. They're a liability. We should have&nbsp;&nbsp; nothing to do with them. We have lots of friends&nbsp; in the Middle East. We get along with Jordan,&nbsp;&nbsp; we get along with Egypt—we don't need Israel. This&nbsp; tiny little country with no natural resources at&nbsp;&nbsp; all. Egypt's far more important.Anyway, that's&nbsp; just—so okay, let's talk about the South Africa&nbsp;&nbsp; story. I'm fascinated by this. So basically these&nbsp; Palestinians are reporting—well this happened,&nbsp;&nbsp; right? So basically these Gazans were told, hey,&nbsp; I know how you can get out if you want to get out.&nbsp;&nbsp; You've got to give me $1,600 in crypto per person&nbsp; in your family who wants to leave and then show up&nbsp;&nbsp; at a certain point and we've got it all figured&nbsp; out. So for the people who decided to bite on&nbsp;&nbsp; this—which probably wasn't most of them because&nbsp; there's so many scammers, so many lying sacks of&nbsp;&nbsp; [ __ ] in a war-zone situation like that—that's a&nbsp; serious leap of faith that most people wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp; take. But some people did. And for those people&nbsp; they ended up being put on a bus and routed down&nbsp;&nbsp; to Khan Younis and then taken to the border&nbsp; crossing and waved through by Israeli troops.&nbsp;&nbsp; And the Israeli troops, finally, right before&nbsp; they left the occupied territory of Gaza—this&nbsp;&nbsp; is very sinister—the Israelis said you have to&nbsp; leave all of your belongings behind. Then, just&nbsp;&nbsp; with the clothes on their backs… very familiar,&nbsp; very 1942. And they then got into Israel proper,&nbsp;&nbsp; they were taken to the airport and then flown.&nbsp; They didn't know where they were going. This one&nbsp;&nbsp; group flew to Kenya, to Nairobi, and then they&nbsp; were stuck for ten hours on the airport tarmac.&nbsp;&nbsp; They have no idea where they're going. This&nbsp; is also familiar. It reminds me of the ship&nbsp;&nbsp; the “St. Louis” in World War II that's flying&nbsp; around. But maybe this was all part of the plan.&nbsp;&nbsp; This obscure charity that appears to be a front&nbsp; organization for the Israeli government or for&nbsp;&nbsp; the Mossad or both arranged all this, and they&nbsp; found themselves in the Republic of South Africa.&nbsp;&nbsp; South Africa has long historic ties supporting&nbsp; the Palestinians for obvious political and&nbsp;&nbsp; cultural reasons. And so the South Africans—this&nbsp; has been going on for a while. And now the foreign&nbsp;&nbsp; minister, Ronald Lamola, finally became aware of&nbsp; it and he's like wait, wait, wait, we see what&nbsp;&nbsp; you're doing here. You're ethnically cleansing the&nbsp; Gaza Strip. Drip by drip by drip. And there's been&nbsp;&nbsp; a lot of flights to other countries in Africa and&nbsp; possibly other continents as well. This is what&nbsp;&nbsp; it looks like, right?It does. And this is exactly&nbsp; what the Egyptians have been warning against since&nbsp;&nbsp; this whole thing started: yes, they love their&nbsp; Palestinian brothers, yes they want to help,&nbsp;&nbsp; but to open the border and allow Palestinians to&nbsp; come across the border into Sinai would do nothing&nbsp;&nbsp; but abet Israel's policy of ethnic cleansing. You&nbsp; can't do it. Because as soon as that happens the&nbsp;&nbsp; Israelis are like, well what can you do? It's&nbsp; empty now. You want to eat that or what? If you&nbsp;&nbsp; won't, we will—and then they move in and it's a&nbsp; fait accompli. I mean that's already what they've&nbsp;&nbsp; done to 60% of the West Bank. Exactly. It's so—I&nbsp; gotta say I admire this level of cynicism. It's&nbsp;&nbsp; amazing.It is. And you know one of the things&nbsp; that's pleasantly surprising to me—and don't&nbsp;&nbsp; take this the wrong way because I don't mean that&nbsp; Palestinians should suffer more than they have&nbsp;&nbsp; already—but it's good and it's important for these&nbsp; countries to stand up to the Israelis finally.&nbsp;&nbsp; Stand up to them and say no.Yeah, for sure. Thanks&nbsp; for the donations over on YouTube guys, much&nbsp;&nbsp; appreciate the super chats, $4.99 from Morrow.&nbsp; Really appreciate that. And thanks for tuning in.&nbsp;&nbsp; Alex Bruce also $4.99. Thank you very much. He's&nbsp; a Christian who leans conservative but greatly&nbsp;&nbsp; appreciates our insights. Thank you very much.&nbsp; Hope we're not blurry on my YouTube. We're blurry,&nbsp;&nbsp; but I don't know. Mine looks pretty good. Okay,&nbsp; so that was probably that packet issue that Robby&nbsp;&nbsp; filled us in about yesterday.Yeah, so I guess that&nbsp; puts an end to it. I mean what happens to these&nbsp;&nbsp; people when they get to South Africa with just the&nbsp; clothes on their back? One of the little girls in&nbsp;&nbsp; the family said she loved being able to go to the&nbsp; store and buy things, something they couldn't do&nbsp;&nbsp; in Gaza. Obviously someone's giving them money so&nbsp; that that can happen. So there must be a support&nbsp;&nbsp; infrastructure there. I really fear that they're&nbsp; going to be forcibly returned. I can't imagine&nbsp;&nbsp; that. I hope not. I seriously can't imagine the&nbsp; South Africans doing that. They've been such a&nbsp;&nbsp; good friend to the Palestinian people at the&nbsp; ICJ and everything. I just don't see it.Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp; trying to look and see if there's any last-second&nbsp; questions or comments that we need to get to&nbsp;&nbsp; before we say sayonara until tomorrow.Okay, good.&nbsp; We don't look blurry. Okay, quick, very quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp; John, off-topic question. Have you been following&nbsp; Kuwaiti news at all? If so, any thoughts?I have&nbsp;&nbsp; been following Kuwaiti news as a matter of fact.&nbsp; There is a very serious, significant split in&nbsp;&nbsp; the Kuwaiti royal family that has spilled&nbsp; into the media. And I'm worried about it&nbsp;&nbsp; because the Kuwaiti response has been to dissolve&nbsp; parliament, which isn't going to help the split in&nbsp;&nbsp; their family any. We'll see where it goes.Okay,&nbsp; well I have no—I was not asked that question and&nbsp;&nbsp; I'm glad because I don't have an answer for that&nbsp; question.Guys, thank you so much for tuning into&nbsp;&nbsp; the program with Ted Rall and John Kiriakou. We&nbsp; will be back tomorrow, Wednesday—sorry, 9 a.m.&nbsp;&nbsp; Eastern time. We're here Monday through Friday.&nbsp; Please like, follow and share the show. Please&nbsp;&nbsp; stay tuned to the TMI show with me, Ted Rall&nbsp; and Manila Chan coming up right after this, and&nbsp;&nbsp; take care and see you tomorrow.John, take care and&nbsp; talk to you soon. Bye-bye, Ted. Bye. Take care.


